{
  "id": "7eausbl",
  "title": "Chapter 15: Diseases and Immunity",
  "order": 64,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "e8z21ie",
      "content": "Diseases are abnormal conditions that affect the normal functioning of the body. They may be caused by pathogens such as bacteria, viruses, fungi and parasites, or due to nutritional deficiencies and lifestyle factors. The human immune system protects the body against disease-causing organisms."
    },
    {
      "type": "statStrip",
      "stats": [
        {
          "label": "Disease Types",
          "value": "Communicable & Non-Communicable",
          "icon": "info"
        },
        {
          "label": "Immunity Categories",
          "value": "Innate & Acquired",
          "icon": "check"
        },
        {
          "label": "Primary Prevention",
          "value": "Vaccination",
          "icon": "award"
        }
      ],
      "id": "8tx6iqp"
    },
    {
      "id": "e8lj4bt",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Meaning of Disease"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Diseases are broadly classified into communicable (infectious) and non-communicable (non-infectious) categories.",
        "Deficiency diseases occur due to a lack of essential nutrients, vitamins, or minerals in the daily diet.",
        "The immune system provides protection through innate (present from birth) and acquired (developed during lifetime) immunity.",
        "Vaccination induces active immunity by introducing antigens to stimulate antibody production without causing severe illness."
      ],
      "id": "1kxraz9"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "xhbtuk9",
      "content": "• A disease is a condition that impairs normal body function."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "l28qidn",
      "content": "• It may cause pain, weakness, discomfort or dysfunction of organs."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "lhwkub4",
      "content": "• Diseases may be infectious or non‑infectious."
    },
    {
      "id": "6wawsqz",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "2. Types of Diseases"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "y6aito4",
      "content": "• Communicable diseases – Spread from one person to another."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "vthr83v",
      "content": "• Non‑communicable diseases – Do not spread between individuals."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "d3b9e2o",
      "content": "• Deficiency diseases – Caused by lack of nutrients."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "aeb0iy7",
      "content": "• Hereditary diseases – Passed through genes."
    },
    {
